// HARD Question Bank - Domain 2.0 (part B: 2.4-2.5)
window.HARD_BANK = (window.HARD_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 2.4 (30) ----------
{o:"2.4",q:"An identity provider shows 1,200 failed logins in 30 minutes: 600 different accounts, each with exactly 2 attempts, all using either 'Winter2027!' or 'Company123!'. Account lockout is set at 5 failures. Which attack is this, and why did lockout not stop it?",c:["Brute force; the lockout policy was not enforced on the identity provider","Credential stuffing; the passwords came from a prior breach dump","Password spraying; each account stayed under the lockout threshold","Dictionary attack; hashes were cracked offline after a database theft"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A brute-force attack hammers one account with many guesses, and here lockout is configured at 5, so no account ever reached it. It would be the answer if a single account showed thousands of failures or lockout were disabled.","Incorrect. Credential stuffing replays distinct leaked username and password pairs, so each account would show its own password. Here only two shared passwords are tried across 600 accounts. It would fit if each user had a unique, breached password.","Correct. Two common passwords tried a couple of times on each of many accounts is spraying, and it deliberately stays below the per-account lockout of 5. Detect it by counting failures across accounts from one source rather than per account.","Incorrect. An offline dictionary attack runs against stolen hashes and generates no failed logins at the IdP. It would be the answer if the evidence were a leaked password database and no online authentication failures."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Logins succeed on the first try for 40 users from a botnet, and every username is paired with a different password. Most of those users reused passwords from a 2024 retail breach. Which attack is this, and what is the BEST control?",c:["Credential stuffing; MFA plus breached-password screening","Password spraying; longer lockout durations and a lower threshold","Brute force; CAPTCHA challenges and per-IP rate limiting","Pass-the-hash; disabling NTLM and enabling Credential Guard"],a:0,e:["Correct. Valid leaked pairs succeed immediately wherever passwords are reused, which is why first-try successes with unique passwords point to stuffing. MFA blunts a correct password, and screening blocks known-breached ones.","Incorrect. Spraying tries the same few passwords against many accounts and mostly fails. Lockout tuning would not help when the first attempt is already correct. It fits when many accounts show repeated failures with identical guesses.","Incorrect. Brute force is guessing, so it produces many failures per account, not first-try successes. CAPTCHA and rate limits help with guessing volume but not with valid credentials. It fits when failures dominate the logs.","Incorrect. Pass-the-hash reuses captured NTLM hashes on internal systems and does not involve breached web passwords. Disabling NTLM would not affect this internet-facing login. It fits internal lateral movement with stolen hashes."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A SIEM correlates the following for one user: login from Dallas at 08:02, login from Warsaw at 08:15, then a 4 GB download from SharePoint at 08:20, about 50 times the user's baseline. Which pair of indicators is present?",c:["Concurrent session usage and blocked content","Resource inaccessibility and out-of-cycle logging","Missing logs and repeated account lockouts","Impossible travel with abnormal data volume"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The sessions are 13 minutes apart in different cities rather than overlapping, and nothing was blocked by a filter. Concurrent sessions would fit two active sessions at the same time, with a proxy or DLP denial logged.","Incorrect. The user reached SharePoint without trouble and the logins are not at odd local hours in the stem. It would fit if services were unreachable and activity occurred at a time the user never works.","Incorrect. There is no gap in the logs and no failed attempts. Missing logs suggest tampering, and lockouts suggest guessing. Neither is described here.","Correct. Dallas to Warsaw in 13 minutes is physically impossible for one person, and a 50x download volume is an anomalous data movement. Together they suggest a compromised account staging data."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"On the host itself, an analyst finds the local Windows Security log is empty from 01:00 to 03:30, with event 1102 (the audit log was cleared) at 03:31 from an admin session that started at 03:29. What is the MOST likely interpretation?",c:["Routine log rotation after the Security log reached its maximum size","Log clearing by an attacker; treat it as an indicator of compromise","Time synchronization drift that shifted event timestamps by several hours","Log forwarding failure to the SIEM caused by a collector outage"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Rotation or archiving under size limits does not log a manual clear event from an interactive admin session. It would fit an archived log file appearing on schedule with no 1102 event.","Correct. Event 1102 means someone cleared the log, and the timing right after a new admin session starts looks like track covering. Preserve evidence, review that session, and treat it as an indicator of compromise.","Incorrect. Clock drift moves timestamps, but it does not delete events or create an audit log cleared entry. It would fit events appearing out of order across hosts.","Incorrect. A forwarding failure creates a gap in the SIEM while the local log remains intact. Here the local log is the one that is empty. It would fit when local events exist but the SIEM lacks them."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A web server shows 90% CPU from a process named 'kworkerds' making outbound connections to a mining pool on port 3333. No files are encrypted and no accounts show unusual logins. Which malware type and indicator are MOST likely?",c:["Ransomware; blocked content and file encryption","Rootkit; impossible travel and missing logs","Cryptojacking; sustained resource consumption","Logic bomb; out-of-cycle logging at a trigger time"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Ransomware encrypts files and shows ransom notes or blocked access to data. Nothing here is encrypted. It would be the answer if documents were renamed and unreadable.","Incorrect. A rootkit conceals itself and would not advertise 90% CPU and pool traffic, and there are no login anomalies for impossible travel. It would fit hidden drivers with log gaps.","Correct. A disguised process name, saturated CPU and a connection to a mining pool port are the signature of cryptojacking. The indicator is resource consumption.","Incorrect. A logic bomb stays dormant until a date or condition fires and then performs a destructive action. This process is continuously mining. It would fit a payload that triggers on a specific date."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Malware spread from one unpatched host to 300 others via the EternalBlue SMB exploit within an hour, with no user interaction. Which type is it, and what containment step comes FIRST?",c:["Trojan; restore from backups before anything else","Virus; run full antivirus scans on all 300 hosts first","Remote access Trojan; block its C2 domain at the DNS server","Worm; isolate affected subnets and block lateral SMB (445)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A Trojan needs the user to run a disguised program, which did not occur, and restoring before containment invites immediate reinfection. Restoration belongs in the recovery phase.","Incorrect. A virus attaches to files and needs a user or host action to execute. Scanning 300 hosts while the spread continues is slow and does not contain it. Scanning fits eradication after containment.","Incorrect. A RAT arrives through user execution and provides remote control. Blocking its C2 does not stop self-propagation over SMB. It would fit a single compromised host with an outbound beacon.","Correct. Exploit-driven self-replication with no user action is a worm. Containment comes first, so isolate the affected subnets and block SMB on 445 between hosts to stop further spread."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"EDR shows WINWORD.EXE spawning powershell.exe -enc <base64>, which downloads code straight into memory and establishes WMI-based persistence. No new executables appear on disk. Which malware class is this, and what is the BEST detection approach?",c:["Fileless malware; behavior-based EDR and script block logging","Boot sector virus; signature-based antivirus scans of the MBR","Polymorphic virus; heuristic scanning of executable files on disk","Rootkit; offline scans from trusted bootable media"],a:0,e:["Correct. Office spawning encoded PowerShell with in-memory payloads and WMI persistence is living off the land. With no files to scan, behavioral detection and script block logging reveal it.","Incorrect. Nothing here touches the boot sector, and a signature scan of the MBR would find nothing. It would fit a threat that loads before the operating system starts.","Incorrect. Polymorphic viruses mutate their code on disk to evade signatures, but this activity leaves no files to scan. It would fit changing binaries that continue to be written to disk.","Incorrect. A rootkit hides components from the running OS, which is discovered by offline scanning. This chain is visible in EDR process telemetry. Offline scans would fit hidden drivers that live-OS tools cannot see."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A server's Task Manager and installed antivirus show nothing unusual, but a scan from trusted bootable media finds hidden kernel drivers and files that the running OS does not list. Which malware type is MOST likely?",c:["Worm that spreads using a hidden network service","Rootkit that masks its components from the running OS","Spyware that captures keystrokes through a browser hook","Logic bomb that stays dormant until a trigger fires"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A worm replicates over the network and would produce traffic and infected neighbours, not objects visible only to offline scans. It would fit rapid propagation between hosts.","Correct. Hiding drivers and files from the live OS while appearing only under an offline scan is the defining behavior of a rootkit. Trusted-boot or offline scanning is how it is exposed.","Incorrect. Spyware steals data, and a browser hook would be visible in the process list and to the antivirus. It would fit data captured from user input without any hidden kernel objects.","Incorrect. A logic bomb is defined by its trigger condition, not by concealment from the OS. It would fit a payload that fires on a date or when an account is removed."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A company sees 50 Gbps of DNS responses arriving from thousands of open resolvers it never queried, saturating its internet link. Which attack is this, and what is the BEST mitigation?",c:["SYN flood; enable SYN cookies on the edge firewall","DNS cache poisoning; deploy DNSSEC on the authoritative zone","Smurf attack; disable directed broadcasts on border routers","DNS amplification DDoS; upstream scrubbing or ISP filtering"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A SYN flood exhausts the connection table with half-open TCP handshakes, not an inbound wave of unsolicited UDP DNS responses. It would fit a server running out of backlog slots.","Incorrect. Cache poisoning forges records in a resolver to redirect victims, and DNSSEC addresses that. Here nothing is wrong with the answers, only the volume. It would fit users being sent to false IPs.","Incorrect. Smurf uses spoofed ICMP echo requests sent to broadcast addresses. The traffic here is DNS from open resolvers. It would fit ICMP replies from a network's broadcast address.","Correct. Spoofed queries make open resolvers send large responses at the victim. A volumetric flood fills the link, so only upstream scrubbing or ISP-level filtering can help."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Traffic analysis shows responses from legitimate third-party servers arriving at your IP, each roughly the same size as the request that triggered it. Which term is MOST precise for this DDoS?",c:["Amplification attack using a high amplification factor","Reflected DDoS without meaningful amplification","On-path attack intercepting traffic between client and server","Replay attack resending previously captured packets"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Amplification requires responses much larger than the requests, such as DNS or NTP monlist. Here sizes are about equal. It would be the answer if a small query produced a very large response.","Correct. Third parties are tricked by spoofed source addresses into sending replies to the victim, which is reflection. Because the response size matches the request, amplification is not the key feature.","Incorrect. An on-path attacker sits between two parties and reads or alters traffic. Here unsolicited responses simply arrive at the target. It would fit a middlebox altering a live session.","Incorrect. Replay resends valid captured messages to be accepted again, typically for authentication. These packets are fresh replies to spoofed requests. It would fit an old login message being accepted twice."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A switch shows the default gateway's IP now mapped to a workstation's MAC address, and users get HTTPS certificate warnings on internal sites. Which attack is this, and which feature mitigates it?",c:["MAC flooding; port security with a maximum MAC count","VLAN hopping; disable DTP and prune unused trunks","ARP poisoning (on-path); Dynamic ARP Inspection","DHCP starvation; DHCP snooping rate limits on access ports"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. MAC flooding overflows the switch CAM table so it fails open and floods frames. It does not remap a gateway's IP to a MAC. Port security fits that attack.","Incorrect. VLAN hopping uses DTP or double tagging to cross VLANs. Nothing in the stem shows traffic crossing VLANs. Disabling DTP fits that scenario.","Correct. Spoofed ARP replies bind the gateway IP to the attacker's MAC, placing them on-path, which triggers the certificate warnings. DAI validates ARP packets against DHCP snooping bindings.","Incorrect. DHCP starvation exhausts the address pool, typically leaving clients without leases. Here the gateway mapping is the problem. Snooping rate limits fit pool exhaustion and rogue DHCP."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A client and server that both support TLS 1.3 end up negotiating TLS 1.0 after an attacker tampers with the handshake. What is the BEST mitigation against this version downgrade?",c:["Disable TLS 1.0 and 1.1 and enforce TLS_FALLBACK_SCSV downgrade protection","Increase the RSA key length to 4096 bits and reissue the server certificate","Deploy HSTS with a one-year max-age and submit the domain for browser preloading","Pin the server certificate in the client application and reject any other chain"],a:0,e:["Correct. If old versions are not enabled they cannot be negotiated, and the fallback signaling and TLS 1.3 downgrade sentinels let endpoints detect a tampered handshake.","Incorrect. A longer RSA key strengthens the certificate, but the attacker is forcing a weaker protocol version, not breaking the key. Longer keys fit concerns about factoring or key strength.","Incorrect. HSTS forces browsers to use HTTPS instead of HTTP, which prevents SSL stripping. It does not stop negotiation of an old TLS version. It fits clients being redirected to plain HTTP.","Incorrect. Pinning prevents acceptance of fraudulent certificates, but the real certificate is still valid on a downgraded session. It fits rogue CA or forged certificate attacks."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker extracts a valid Kerberos TGT from a compromised host's memory and presents it from another machine to access resources as the victim, without knowing any password or hash. Which attack is this?",c:["Pass-the-hash using a captured NTLM hash","Golden ticket forged with the KRBTGT key","Kerberoasting of service account tickets","Pass-the-ticket using a stolen TGT"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Pass-the-hash authenticates with an NTLM hash rather than a Kerberos ticket. It would be the answer if the attacker dumped hashes from LSASS and used NTLM authentication.","Incorrect. A golden ticket is forged offline using the KRBTGT account hash, not stolen from a victim's memory. It would fit tickets created for arbitrary users with extended lifetimes.","Incorrect. Kerberoasting requests service tickets and cracks them offline to recover service account passwords. Here the stolen ticket is used directly, with no cracking.","Correct. The attacker reuses a legitimate, stolen Kerberos ticket on another system to impersonate the user. This is pass-the-ticket."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker holding the KRBTGT account hash creates TGTs for any user with any group memberships, valid for years. Which attack is this, and what is the key remediation?",c:["Silver ticket; rotate the targeted service account password","Golden ticket; reset the KRBTGT password twice in sequence","Pass-the-hash; disable NTLM and require SMB signing","Kerberoasting; move service accounts to gMSAs"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A silver ticket is a forged service ticket signed with one service account's key and is limited to that service. Rotating that password would fix it, but the KRBTGT hash exposure here is domain-wide.","Correct. A forged TGT signed with the KRBTGT key is a golden ticket. Resetting the KRBTGT password twice invalidates tickets signed with both the current and previous keys.","Incorrect. Pass-the-hash replays NTLM hashes, while this scenario forges Kerberos tickets. Disabling NTLM would not invalidate them. It fits hash reuse for lateral movement.","Incorrect. Kerberoasting cracks service ticket hashes offline, and gMSAs protect against that. It does not describe forging TGTs with a stolen KRBTGT key."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A captured authentication message is resent 10 minutes later and accepted by a legacy protocol that has no freshness checks. Which attack is this, and what is the fix?",c:["Downgrade attack; disable TLS 1.0 on the legacy service","Hash collision; migrate message digests from MD5 to SHA-256","Replay; add nonces or timestamps and session binding","Brute force; enforce account lockout after repeated failures"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A downgrade forces weaker protocol versions during negotiation. Here a valid message is simply resent. Disabling old TLS versions fits version negotiation tampering.","Incorrect. A collision means two inputs yielding the same hash. The old message was accepted as-is, with no hash manipulation involved. Stronger digests fit forged document or signature scenarios.","Correct. With nothing proving a message is new, a captured valid message is accepted again. Nonces, timestamps and session binding make each message single-use.","Incorrect. Brute force guesses credentials and generates failed attempts. No guessing occurred here, so lockout would not stop the replay. It fits repeated failures against an account."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Which scenario BEST describes a birthday attack?",c:["Using a table of precomputed hashes to reverse unsalted passwords","Resending a captured session token before it expires","Guessing passwords based on a user's date of birth and name","Finding two inputs with the same hash faster than brute force"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. That describes a rainbow table attack, which trades storage for speed against unsalted hashes. Salting defeats it. It is unrelated to collision probability.","Incorrect. That is a replay or session hijacking attack. It reuses a valid token instead of exploiting hash collision probability.","Incorrect. That is a targeted password guessing attack using personal information. Despite the name, a birthday attack has nothing to do with dates of birth.","Correct. The birthday paradox means collisions appear after roughly the square root of the possible hash space, so weak or short hashes such as MD5 are much easier to collide."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A proxy log shows denied requests from a workstation to domains categorized 'Newly Registered' every 5 minutes, including overnight when no user is logged in. What is the MOST likely explanation?",c:["Malware beaconing to C2 domains that the proxy blocks","A browser extension prefetching advertisements in the background","An OS update agent checking the vendor's content delivery network","A typo-squatting redirect from the user's most recent search"],a:0,e:["Correct. Fixed-interval requests to freshly registered domains with no user present are beaconing. The blocked content is a useful indicator, so isolate the host and investigate.","Incorrect. Extensions prefetch while a user has a browser open, and ad domains are rarely newly registered. It would fit activity that stops when the browser closes.","Incorrect. Update agents contact established vendor domains that are categorized and allowed. It would fit regular traffic to known vendor CDNs.","Incorrect. A typo-squatting redirect requires a user to navigate, and would not recur on a fixed interval overnight. It would fit one-off hits right after a mistyped URL."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A login form backed by a directory service receives the input username=admin)(|(password=*). Which injection is this?",c:["SQL injection against the login query","XML injection through a SOAP request body","LDAP injection into the directory search filter","Command injection through a shell-invoking function"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. SQL injection uses quotes, comments, and keywords such as OR or UNION against a database query. The parentheses here are not SQL syntax. It would fit a payload like ' OR 1=1 --.","Incorrect. XML injection inserts tags or entities into an XML document. There are no angle brackets or entity definitions in this input.","Correct. Parentheses, the pipe for OR, and a wildcard manipulate an LDAP search filter so the password condition always matches. Input should be escaped and validated before filter construction.","Incorrect. Command injection appends shell operators such as semicolons, pipes to commands, or backticks. This input contains filter syntax, not shell operators."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A PDF-generation service accepts a JSON field url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/ and returns the fetched content. Which attack is this, and what is the target?",c:["Cross-site request forgery (CSRF) abusing an admin's authenticated browser session","Server-side request forgery (SSRF) targeting cloud instance metadata credentials","Directory traversal targeting files outside the web root of the PDF service","XML external entity (XXE) injection targeting local files through the parser"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. CSRF tricks a user's browser into sending an authenticated request. Here the server itself makes the request. It would fit a malicious page submitting a form to a site where the victim is logged in.","Correct. The server is tricked into requesting an internal address, and 169.254.169.254 is the cloud metadata service, which can return temporary IAM credentials. Mitigate with allow lists and IMDSv2.","Incorrect. Traversal uses ../ sequences to read local files. This input is a URL pointing to a network resource, not a path.","Incorrect. XXE abuses external entity declarations in XML. The input here is a JSON field containing a URL, with no XML parsing involved."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A user with Helpdesk rights changes a URL from /user/1005/edit to /user/1001/edit and modifies the CEO's email address, although Helpdesk should only manage its own region. Which vulnerability was exploited?",c:["SQL injection through the numeric user ID in the URL path","Cross-site scripting stored in the user profile record","Session replay of a captured helpdesk cookie","Insecure direct object reference (broken access control)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SQL injection requires attacker-supplied query syntax in the parameter. Only a different valid ID was substituted. It would fit quotes or operators that change the database query.","Incorrect. Stored XSS places script that runs in other users' browsers. No script was injected, only a record was altered. It would fit unexpected pop-ups when viewing profiles.","Incorrect. Replay reuses a captured credential or token, and the attacker here was already authenticated as themselves. It would fit a stolen cookie used from another device.","Correct. The application accepted a direct reference to another object without checking that the caller was authorized for it. Enforce object-level authorization server-side."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A service account begins querying every user's SPN and requesting TGS tickets, and the tickets are later cracked offline. Which attack is this?",c:["Kerberoasting of accounts that have SPNs registered","AS-REP roasting of accounts without Kerberos pre-authentication","Silver ticket forging of service tickets with a service key","Pass-the-hash with NTLM hashes dumped from LSASS"],a:0,e:["Correct. Any domain user can request service tickets for SPN accounts, which are encrypted with the service account's key and can be cracked offline. Use long random passwords or gMSAs.","Incorrect. AS-REP roasting targets accounts with pre-authentication disabled by requesting AS-REP responses, not TGS tickets for SPNs. It would fit queries for users flagged without pre-auth.","Incorrect. A silver ticket is forged by someone who already has a service account's key. Here the attacker is trying to obtain that secret by cracking.","Incorrect. Pass-the-hash reuses NTLM hashes without cracking and does not involve requesting service tickets. It would fit hash dumps and immediate lateral logons."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Multiple users report MFA push notifications they did not initiate at 2 a.m., and one approved a prompt to make it stop. Which attack occurred, and which control BEST prevents it?",c:["SIM swapping; replacing SMS codes with app-based authenticators","MFA fatigue (push bombing); number matching and rate limits","Adversary-in-the-middle phishing; hardware-bound FIDO2 keys","Token theft; shorter session lifetimes and conditional access"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. SIM swapping hijacks a phone number to intercept SMS codes, and these are app pushes. It would fit codes arriving at the attacker's SIM.","Correct. Repeated unsolicited prompts wear down a user until they approve. Number matching forces proof of presence at the login screen, and rate limits cap prompt volume.","Incorrect. AiTM requires the user to visit a proxy phishing page and enter credentials, so the user initiates the login. Here prompts arrived unprompted. FIDO2 fits that proxy-phishing case.","Incorrect. Token theft steals an already issued session token after authentication. These events are fresh prompts. Shorter sessions fit stolen cookies reused from another host."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker ports a victim's phone number to a new SIM and receives their SMS OTP codes. Which mitigation removes the dependence on the phone number?",c:["Replace SMS codes with phishing-resistant FIDO2 passkeys","Extend the SMS OTP length from 6 to 10 digits","Add a carrier port-out PIN while keeping SMS as the second factor","Shorten SMS OTP validity from 10 minutes to 60 seconds"],a:0,e:["Correct. Passkeys and FIDO2 keys are bound to a device and origin, so a hijacked phone number gives the attacker nothing. This removes SMS from the trust chain.","Incorrect. A longer code does not help when the attacker receives the message itself. It would fit an attacker guessing codes.","Incorrect. A port-out PIN makes swapping harder, but SMS stays the second factor and social engineering of carrier staff remains possible. It does not remove the dependence.","Incorrect. A shorter lifetime narrows the window but the attacker receives the code in real time and can use it at once. It fits codes stolen long after issue."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A web server log entry shows: GET /index.php?page=../../../../etc/passwd HTTP/1.1 200 1843. What do the 200 status and 1843-byte response suggest?",c:["The request was blocked by the WAF before reaching the application","The application crashed while parsing the oversized parameter","The payload is a reflected XSS string echoed back to the browser","The traversal succeeded and the response body held file contents"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A WAF block would normally return 403 or a custom block page. A 200 with a substantial body shows the request reached the application.","Incorrect. A crash typically yields a 500 error, a timeout, or no response. A normal 200 response with a body shows the application handled the request.","Incorrect. XSS payloads contain script such as tags or event handlers. This parameter contains ../ path sequences and a system file name.","Correct. A success status with a non-trivial body size after ../ sequences targeting /etc/passwd indicates the file was likely returned. Confirm by reviewing the response and fix by validating file paths."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Which network observation MOST strongly suggests data exfiltration via DNS tunneling?",c:["Thousands of A record lookups for well-known CDN domains during business hours","Periodic UDP 123 exchanges with a public time server","High-entropy subdomains in heavy TXT query volume to one domain","A burst of NXDOMAIN responses for randomly generated names across many domains"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. High A record volume for popular CDN names during work hours is ordinary browsing. It would fit tunneling only if the names were long and unique.","Incorrect. UDP 123 is NTP time synchronization, a normal service. It would fit time synchronization problems, not encoded data in queries.","Correct. Encoded data placed in subdomains and returned through TXT records to one attacker-controlled domain is the classic DNS tunneling pattern.","Incorrect. Many NXDOMAIN answers for random names across domains match a domain generation algorithm looking for live C2 servers. That is C2 discovery, not exfiltration through one domain."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A standard user gains SYSTEM rights by editing a script that a scheduled task runs as SYSTEM, because the script sits in a folder the user can write to. What is the root cause?",c:["SQL injection in the task scheduler's configuration database","Insecure permissions on a script that a SYSTEM task executes","Unquoted service path in the scheduled task's executable name","DLL search-order hijacking in the scheduler service's process"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The scheduler's settings are not being manipulated through database queries. It would fit untrusted input being concatenated into a query.","Correct. A privileged task running a user-writable file lets any user change what executes as SYSTEM. Fix by restricting write permissions to the script and its folder.","Incorrect. Unquoted path flaws involve spaces in a service path that let a planted executable run earlier in the path. Here the script content itself is editable.","Incorrect. DLL hijacking relies on a process loading a library from an attacker-controlled directory. The attacker here changes a script, not a library."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Which attack is MOST associated with RFID badge cloning?",c:["Tailgating behind an employee who badges in at a secured door","Shoulder surfing the PIN entered at a badge-plus-PIN reader","Dumpster diving for discarded badge printing records","Skimming a badge with a proxmark-style reader at short range"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Tailgating gains entry by following someone through a door and needs no copy of the credential. It would be the answer if the attacker simply walked in behind a user.","Incorrect. Shoulder surfing steals a PIN by observation and does not duplicate the badge. It would fit capturing a second factor.","Incorrect. Dumpster diving recovers discarded paper or media. It does not read the radio data from a live badge.","Correct. Many 125 kHz proximity badges transmit a static ID without encryption, so a reader held a few centimeters away can capture and write it to a blank card."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker cuts power to a building's badge system, and the doors default to unlocked. Which weakness did this exploit?",c:["Physical access controls configured to fail open on power loss","Badge readers storing credentials in cleartext on the controller","Door controllers lacking a tamper switch on the enclosure","Visitor logs maintained on paper rather than electronically"],a:0,e:["Correct. Fail-open doors default to unlocked when power is lost. Life-safety codes often require this on egress doors, so compensate with alarms, guards or battery backup.","Incorrect. Cleartext credential storage would let someone read badge data, but it would not unlock doors when power is cut. It fits a stolen controller exposing IDs.","Incorrect. A tamper switch alerts on enclosure access. Power was cut and the doors responded to that state. It would fit someone opening the controller box.","Incorrect. Paper visitor logs reduce auditability but have no connection to door behavior during an outage. They would fit difficulty tracing who entered."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Which log pattern BEST indicates a successful brute-force attack?",c:["A few failures on each of hundreds of accounts from one source","Failures on many accounts from many sources, each with a distinct leaked password","Many failed logins for one account followed by a success from the same source","A successful login from a new country followed by a password reset"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A few attempts across hundreds of accounts is a spraying pattern. It would be the answer if a single account were hammered with guesses.","Incorrect. Distinct leaked passwords from distributed sources describe credential stuffing, where many attempts succeed on first try.","Correct. A burst of failures against one account followed by a success from the same source shows repeated guessing that finally succeeded.","Incorrect. A foreign login followed by a password reset suggests account takeover, perhaps after phishing. It does not show repeated guessing."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A Microsoft-signed rundll32.exe in System32 executes a DLL by full path from a user's temp folder and connects to an unknown external IP. Which technique is MOST likely?",c:["DLL search-order hijacking by an application that loads a planted library","Living-off-the-land abuse of a signed binary to run a malicious DLL","Process hollowing of a suspended svchost.exe instance","Reflective injection of a payload into lsass.exe memory"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Search-order hijacking depends on an application loading a library from a directory it searches by name. Here rundll32 is told the explicit path.","Correct. rundll32 is a trusted signed binary that can run any DLL. Attackers use it to proxy execution and blend in with allow-listed tools. Investigate the DLL and the parent process.","Incorrect. Hollowing starts a legitimate process in a suspended state and replaces its memory with malicious code. Nothing here shows a suspended process or replaced image.","Incorrect. Reflective injection loads code into another process memory, typically to harvest credentials from LSASS. Here a DLL file is loaded from disk by rundll32."]},

// ---------- 2.5 (22) ----------
{o:"2.5",q:"A hospital cannot patch its imaging systems for 6 months because the vendor has not certified the update. Which combination provides the MOST effective interim mitigation?",c:["Install host-based antivirus, allow vendor remote access over a VPN, and review logs monthly","Enable full-disk encryption, restrict logon to local admins, and rotate passwords quarterly","Move them behind a WAF, enable TLS 1.3, and scan weekly for vulnerabilities","Segment them into a restricted VLAN, permit only required flows, and monitor that segment"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Antivirus may be unsupported on certified devices, and a standing vendor VPN adds exposure while monthly log review is slow. These controls do not isolate vulnerable systems.","Incorrect. These are sound hygiene measures, but they do not reduce network reachability of an unpatched service. They fit protecting lost devices and credentials.","Incorrect. A WAF protects web applications only, and these systems run imaging protocols such as DICOM. Scanning finds issues but does not mitigate them.","Correct. Restricting network reachability with tight ACLs and watching the segment is the standard compensating approach when a vulnerable system cannot be patched."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An unattended public kiosk should run only one application. Which approach gives the STRONGEST protection against running unauthorized code?",c:["Application allow listing and kiosk mode lockdown","A deny list of known malware hashes updated daily","Endpoint antivirus with real-time heuristic scanning","Host-based IPS with signature updates from the vendor"],a:0,e:["Correct. Allow listing permits only approved executables, so unknown malware and unapproved tools cannot run. Kiosk mode limits what the interface exposes.","Incorrect. A deny list stops only known-bad hashes, so new or modified samples run. It fits blocking specific known threats on a general-purpose system.","Incorrect. Antivirus is reactive and depends on detection, which new malware can evade. It fits broad coverage on user workstations.","Incorrect. A HIPS relies on signatures and behavior rules and cannot prevent every unapproved executable. It fits detecting known exploit patterns."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An EDR alert shows ransomware actively encrypting files on a file server, with new files being changed every few seconds. What is the FIRST response action?",c:["Eradicate by wiping the server and reinstalling the operating system","Recover by restoring files from the latest immutable backup","Contain by isolating the server from the network","Analyze by submitting a sample to a sandbox to identify the family"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Eradication follows containment and evidence preservation, and wiping now destroys forensic data while encryption of shares may continue. It fits after the spread is stopped.","Incorrect. Restoring into an environment where encryption continues risks encrypting the restored data again. Recovery comes after containment and eradication.","Correct. Encryption is in progress, so stop the damage and lateral spread first by isolating the host, preferably through EDR network containment, then continue the response.","Incorrect. Identifying the family is useful but does not stop ongoing encryption. Analysis fits once the system is contained."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An organization disables SMBv1, removes local administrator rights, and enforces Credential Guard on all endpoints. Which BEST describes these measures collectively?",c:["Decommissioning of legacy protocols to retire unsupported services","Endpoint hardening to reduce attack surface and lateral movement","Risk transference of endpoint exposure to a third-party provider","Detective monitoring to identify compromised accounts after the fact"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Decommissioning retires an asset entirely. Only SMBv1 is turned off here, while admin rights and credential protection are different measures. It fits removing a legacy system.","Correct. Disabling unneeded protocols, removing excess privileges, and protecting credentials are standard hardening that reduces attack surface and credential theft.","Incorrect. Transference shifts financial risk, such as through insurance or outsourcing. These are technical settings applied by the organization itself.","Incorrect. These settings prevent attacks rather than detect them. Detective controls would be alerts and log reviews."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Configuration drift keeps reintroducing insecure settings on servers after administrators make manual changes. Which mitigation is MOST effective at stopping the recurrence?",c:["Quarterly manual audits of server settings against the baseline document","A signed change-management policy forbidding unapproved manual changes","Additional perimeter firewall rules blocking administrative protocols","Automated baseline enforcement with drift detection (GPO or Ansible)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A periodic manual audit finds drift after it has existed for months and does not prevent it. It fits verifying compliance for an auditor.","Incorrect. A policy sets expectations but does not technically reset a changed setting, and the problem is already happening despite administrators knowing the rules. It fits governing change approval.","Incorrect. Perimeter rules control network traffic, not host configuration. They would fit limiting exposure of management ports.","Correct. Continuous enforcement from a baseline automatically returns settings to the secure state and flags drift for review."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Several cloud VMs created outside any tracked project were forgotten and are still running with public IPs. Which mitigation BEST addresses this class of risk?",c:["Asset inventory with a formal decommissioning procedure","Endpoint detection and response agents on all running instances","Weekly credentialed vulnerability scans of the approved production subnets","Cloud access security broker policies for sanctioned applications"],a:0,e:["Correct. You cannot protect what you do not know exists. Discovery, ownership tracking, and a decommissioning process retire unused assets so they stop being exposed.","Incorrect. EDR agents are deployed on known systems and the forgotten VMs have none. EDR fits monitoring managed instances.","Incorrect. Scans cover only the approved subnets, which the forgotten VMs fall outside of. Scanning fits vulnerability management for known assets.","Incorrect. A CASB governs use of SaaS applications and data, not discovery of orphaned infrastructure. It fits shadow IT in cloud services."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A company separates guest Wi-Fi, the corporate LAN, and the PCI cardholder environment so they communicate only through a firewall. What is the PRIMARY benefit of this segmentation for PCI?",c:["It encrypts cardholder data in transit between the zones","It replaces card numbers with tokens so stored PANs fall out of scope","It reduces the number of systems in PCI scope and contains breaches","It substitutes for the quarterly external vulnerability scans from an ASV"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Segmentation restricts reachability but does not encrypt traffic. Encryption is a separate control, such as TLS. It fits protecting data in transit.","Incorrect. Tokenization takes stored card numbers out of scope, but it is a data protection technique and not a result of network separation.","Correct. Properly isolating the cardholder environment narrows what must meet PCI DSS and limits lateral movement toward card data.","Incorrect. External vulnerability scans by an approved scanning vendor are still required regardless of segmentation. It would not replace them."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Which technique BEST prevents a compromised standard user account from installing persistence mechanisms across the whole system?",c:["Full-disk encryption with a pre-boot PIN and TPM binding","Least privilege by removing local administrator rights","Web filtering that blocks newly registered domain categories","A screen lock timeout reduced to two minutes of inactivity"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects data on a lost or stolen device and does nothing once the system is running and logged in. It fits physical theft.","Correct. Without admin rights, a compromised user cannot create services, modify system-wide autoruns, or write to protected locations, which limits persistence.","Incorrect. Web filtering may block downloads, but it does not limit what a compromised account can do on the host. It fits limiting malicious site access.","Incorrect. A screen lock protects an unattended session from physical access. The compromised account here is attacked remotely."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A vendor releases an out-of-band patch for a critical vulnerability being actively exploited. The normal patch cycle is monthly. What is the BEST process?",c:["Hold for the next monthly cycle and add an IPS signature as the interim control","Deploy to all systems at once in the next maintenance window, skipping staged testing","Compensate with a virtual patch on the WAF and defer the update until the vendor confirms stability","Emergency change: test on representative systems, deploy by exposure, keep a backout plan"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Waiting up to a month leaves systems exposed to active exploitation, and an IPS signature may not cover every variant. This fits low-severity patches without known exploitation.","Incorrect. Skipping staged testing risks a faulty patch causing outages everywhere. A rollout without testing or rollback is unsafe even under urgency.","Incorrect. A virtual patch can help in the short term, but leaving the underlying flaw unpatched while actively exploited does not fix it. It fits systems that truly cannot be patched.","Correct. An emergency change balances speed and safety, with quick testing on representative systems, exposure-based prioritization, and a backout plan."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Which monitoring mitigation would BEST detect an attacker using valid credentials at unusual hours from a new country?",c:["User and entity behavior analytics (UEBA) alerting","Signature-based antivirus with daily definition updates","Web application firewall with the OWASP core rule set","Network IPS tuned with vendor exploit signatures"],a:0,e:["Correct. UEBA baselines normal behavior for each user and flags deviations such as odd hours and new locations, which catches misuse of legitimate credentials.","Incorrect. Antivirus examines files for malicious code and cannot judge whether a valid login is suspicious. It fits detecting known malware.","Incorrect. A WAF inspects web requests for attack patterns, and a valid login contains none. It fits injection or XSS attempts.","Incorrect. An IPS matches exploit signatures in traffic, and legitimate authentication does not match exploits. It fits blocking known network exploits."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Which of the following is an example of isolation rather than segmentation?",c:["Placing HR and Finance on separate VLANs behind an inter-VLAN firewall","Hosting the public web servers in a DMZ between two firewalls","Quarantining an infected laptop to a VLAN with no internal access","Defining production, development and guest security zones with firewall policy"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Separating departments into VLANs with controlled traffic between them is segmentation by design, with defined permitted flows.","Incorrect. A DMZ is a segmented zone with controlled connectivity in both directions. It is architectural, not an action on a specific compromised asset.","Correct. Isolation cuts off a specific compromised or high-risk asset, often automatically, so it cannot communicate with internal resources.","Incorrect. Defining zones with firewall policy is segmentation of the network into trust areas, with permitted traffic between them."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Which control is MOST effective against an authorized insider copying an entire customer database?",c:["Full-disk encryption with pre-boot PIN on analyst laptops","Network segmentation placing the database in a restricted VLAN","Least privilege on exports with DLP monitoring of bulk queries","Phishing-resistant MFA on every database login"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Encryption protects data on lost devices, but the insider is authorized and accesses data while the system is unlocked. It fits device theft.","Incorrect. Segmentation limits network paths, but an authorized insider reaches the database through approved paths. It fits blocking unauthorized hosts.","Correct. Restricting who can export data and monitoring large queries or transfers addresses misuse of legitimate access.","Incorrect. MFA confirms identity, and the insider already authenticates legitimately. It fits stopping stolen credentials."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A company hardens web servers by removing default pages, sample applications, and version banners. Which risk does this MOST directly reduce?",c:["Volumetric denial of service that saturates the web tier link","Credential phishing campaigns aimed at site administrators","Malicious insider abuse of web server administrative privileges","Version fingerprinting and exploits against sample-app flaws"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Removing defaults does not reduce traffic volume or capacity. Floods are addressed by scrubbing and rate limiting.","Incorrect. Phishing targets people and credentials, which hardening of web content does not affect. Awareness training and MFA fit this risk.","Incorrect. Insider abuse concerns authorized privileges, not exposed defaults. Least privilege and monitoring fit this risk.","Correct. Banners help attackers fingerprint versions, and default or sample apps often contain known flaws. Removing them shrinks the attack surface."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An administrator configures a deny list that blocks known ransomware file hashes on endpoints. What is the MAIN limitation of this approach?",c:["Modified or new samples with different hashes are not blocked","Legitimate administrative tools sharing a file name are blocked as false positives","Block decisions cannot be logged or forwarded to the SIEM","Enforcement requires an inline WAF in front of each endpoint"],a:0,e:["Correct. A hash matches one exact file, so recompiled, repacked, or new variants bypass the list. Pair hash lists with behavioral detection.","Incorrect. Hash matching is exact and does not depend on file names, so false positives from name overlap are not how this approach fails. That would fit name-based rules.","Incorrect. Endpoint block events can be logged and forwarded like any other security event. The limitation here is coverage, not visibility.","Incorrect. Hash deny lists are enforced by endpoint agents or OS controls. A WAF protects web applications and is unrelated."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Which mitigation BEST protects backups from being encrypted by ransomware running with domain admin rights?",c:["Daily backups to a mapped network share using a dedicated service account","Replicated backups to a second server with a domain-joined agent","Immutable or offline backups with credentials separate from the domain","Higher backup frequency and longer retention on the same storage array"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A mapped share is reachable from the compromised host, and a domain admin can reach it regardless of the service account. It fits a convenient, non-hardened backup design.","Incorrect. A second server joined to the same domain is still under the attacker's control with domain admin rights. Replication does not provide independence.","Correct. Immutability or an offline copy plus separate credentials means domain admin rights cannot modify or delete the backup.","Incorrect. More frequent backups and longer retention on the same array leave everything accessible to the same attacker. It improves recovery points, not protection."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Which of these is a technical control that enforces least privilege dynamically?",c:["An annual access recertification review","Just-in-time elevation through a PAM solution","A mandatory security awareness training program","A mantrap at the data center entrance"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Recertification is a managerial review performed periodically and does not enforce anything in real time. It fits detecting stale access.","Correct. JIT access in a PAM solution grants elevated rights only when needed, for a limited time, and removes them automatically.","Incorrect. Training is an operational control that influences behavior and does not enforce privilege in systems. It fits reducing user error.","Incorrect. A mantrap is a physical control for entry. It does not control logical privileges."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An IoT network is segmented from the corporate LAN, but the devices can still reach the internet on any port. What should be added to reduce botnet and C2 risk?",c:["Additional internal VLANs to separate each device vendor","A NAC policy enforcing 802.1X posture checks on IoT switch ports","Intrusion detection sensors placed on the IoT VLAN only","Egress filtering limited to required destinations and ports"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. More internal VLANs limit lateral movement between devices, but outbound internet access stays open. It fits stopping device-to-device spread.","Incorrect. 802.1X is often unsupported on IoT devices, and posture checks control network entry rather than outbound destinations. It fits managed endpoints.","Incorrect. IDS sensors detect and alert, but they do not restrict what leaves the network. It fits visibility needs, not prevention of outbound C2.","Correct. Limiting outbound traffic to the destinations and ports the devices need blocks C2 channels and botnet participation."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"What is the PRIMARY purpose of encryption as a mitigation technique?",c:["Preserving confidentiality even if other controls fail","Proving the data was not altered after it was stored","Proving which specific user created or approved the data","Keeping data available when storage hardware fails"],a:0,e:["Correct. Encryption protects the data itself, so even if access controls are bypassed or media is stolen, the content stays unreadable without the key.","Incorrect. Proving data is unaltered is integrity, provided by hashing or digital signatures. Encryption alone does not detect changes.","Incorrect. Proving who created data is non-repudiation, provided by digital signatures. Encryption alone does not bind an identity to content.","Incorrect. Availability when hardware fails comes from redundancy such as RAID and replication. Encryption does not improve it."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A new database server will hold customer PII and must be reachable only from two application servers. Which hardening set is the MOST appropriate?",c:["Keep default accounts for vendor support, apply patches, enable encryption and auditing, allow admin access from any VLAN","Remove default accounts, apply patches, allow connections from the whole server VLAN, enable auditing, leave encryption off for performance","Remove default accounts, apply patches, restrict network access to the app servers, enable encryption and auditing","Disable auditing to preserve performance, patch quarterly, restrict access to the app servers, use default ports with encryption"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Default accounts are well-known targets and allowing administration from any VLAN widens exposure, even though patching, encryption and auditing are good. It fails on accounts and access scope.","Incorrect. Opening the database to the whole server VLAN breaks the two-server requirement, and leaving encryption off weakens PII protection. It fails on access scope and confidentiality.","Correct. This removes default credentials, closes known flaws, limits reachability to the two app servers, and adds encryption and audit logging for PII.","Incorrect. Disabling auditing removes accountability, and quarterly patching leaves long exposure windows, even though access is restricted. It fails on logging and patch cadence."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Which measure is MOST effective at stopping lateral movement that uses stolen administrator credentials?",c:["Password length of 20 characters for all administrative accounts","Tiered administration with separate admin accounts per tier","Phishing-resistant MFA on the remote access VPN gateway","Web filtering that blocks credential-harvesting categories"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Long passwords resist cracking but do nothing once a credential or hash has been stolen and is replayed. It fits resisting offline cracking.","Correct. Tiering keeps high-privilege credentials from being exposed on lower-trust systems, so stealing one does not provide access across the domain.","Incorrect. VPN MFA protects remote entry, but lateral movement happens inside the network after access. It fits stopping external logins with stolen passwords.","Incorrect. Web filtering reduces harvesting by phishing sites, but it does not restrict where already-stolen credentials work. It fits preventing initial credential theft."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Why is monitoring considered a mitigation technique alongside preventive controls?",c:["Monitoring can substitute for preventive controls once alerting is well tuned","Monitoring is required mainly to satisfy audit and compliance obligations","Monitoring stops attacks before they reach the network perimeter","Prevention can fail, so monitoring cuts dwell time for what gets through"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Detection does not stop an attack by itself, so it supplements prevention in a defense-in-depth design rather than replacing it.","Incorrect. Compliance is a driver, but the security value is in finding intrusions earlier and limiting damage. It does not explain monitoring as mitigation.","Incorrect. Monitoring observes activity and alerts, while blocking before the perimeter is a preventive function such as firewalls and filtering.","Correct. No preventive control is perfect, and monitoring reduces dwell time so incidents are contained sooner and damage is limited."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A company must keep a legacy Windows 7 PC to run lab equipment that has no supported replacement. Which approach is BEST?",c:["Isolate it on a dedicated no-internet network with USB restricted and minimal connections","Place it in the DMZ with a host firewall and limited internet access for vendor updates","Join it to the domain with a hardened GPO and an EDR agent as a compensating control","Virtualize it on the production hypervisor cluster with NAT to the user VLAN and a snapshot schedule"],a:0,e:["Correct. An unpatchable legacy system needs strict isolation, no internet, controlled removable media, and minimal allowed connections.","Incorrect. A DMZ is internet-adjacent, and Windows 7 no longer receives updates, so internet access increases exposure for no benefit. It fits supported public-facing servers.","Incorrect. Domain membership exposes the network to a system with unfixed flaws, and modern EDR agents often do not support Windows 7. It fits supported endpoints.","Incorrect. Virtualization may not work with lab hardware, and NAT to the user VLAN keeps the vulnerable OS reachable from users. It fits migrating a supported workload."]}
]);
